WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.088
I had seen in the past complex deals,

00:00:10.088 --> 00:00:12.215
but I'd never seen anything like this.

00:00:14.342 --> 00:00:16.720
And it involved two of Europe's

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.763
leading financial institutions.

00:00:18.763 --> 00:00:19.681
On the one hand,

00:00:19.681 --> 00:00:20.724
the world's oldest bank

00:00:20.724 --> 00:00:23.393
and Italy's third largest
lender Monte Paschi.

00:00:24.978 --> 00:00:27.021
And on the other hand, Deutsche Bank,

00:00:27.021 --> 00:00:30.942
once the world's largest
financial institution.

00:00:30.942 --> 00:00:34.446
The idea that these two had got together

00:00:34.446 --> 00:00:37.824
to cook the books was just astonishing.

00:00:37.824 --> 00:00:41.411
I thought it was clever but illegal.

00:00:42.328 --> 00:00:45.165
And a few of them should
actually go to jail, in my view.

00:00:47.459 --> 00:00:50.378
This was clearly a huge scandal

00:00:50.378 --> 00:00:55.383
that made 450 million dollars disappear
from Monte Paschi's books.

00:00:55.425 --> 00:00:57.761
If it wasn't for our reporting,

00:00:57.761 --> 00:00:59.554
the public may never have found out.

00:01:10.732 --> 00:01:12.442
My name is Elisa Martinuzzi.

00:01:12.442 --> 00:01:14.819
I'm a columnist with Bloomberg Opinion.

00:01:15.361 --> 00:01:17.655
&lt;font color=#F6EAABFF&gt;A presidential address to the nation.&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:17.655 --> 00:01:21.284
&lt;font color=#FFF7B4FF&gt;This is an extraordinary&lt;/font&gt;
&lt;font color=#FFF7B4FF&gt;period for America's economy.&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:21.284 --> 00:01:22.368
&lt;font color=#FFF7B4FF&gt;Over the past few weeks&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:22.368 --> 00:01:24.329
&lt;font color=#FFF7B4FF&gt;many Americans have felt anxiety&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:26.706
&lt;font color=#FFF7B4FF&gt;about their finances and their future.&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:26.706 --> 00:01:28.082
So it's December 2008,

00:01:28.082 --> 00:01:30.960
and we are right in the depth
of the financial crisis.

00:01:32.128 --> 00:01:34.339
&lt;font color=#F8EFB7FF&gt;The Dow tumbled more than 500 points&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:34.339 --> 00:01:37.509
&lt;font color=#F8EFB7FF&gt;after two pillars of the Street&lt;/font&gt;
&lt;font color=#F8EFB7FF&gt;tumbled over the weekend.&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:37.509 --> 00:01:40.970
Financial institutions are
really fighting for survival

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.056
and these two in particular,

00:01:43.056 --> 00:01:45.600
Monte Paschi and Deutsche Bank.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.434
&lt;font color=#FFFBC6FF&gt;We're seeing the stock falling&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:46.434 --> 00:01:48.561
&lt;font color=#FFFBC6FF&gt;by more than 7% as we speak right now.&lt;/font&gt;

00:01:48.561 --> 00:01:50.230
On the one hand, you have Deutsche Bank

00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:52.315
with a massive balance sheet.

00:01:52.315 --> 00:01:54.359
2.5 trillion euros,

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:56.402
it's a size of a large economy.

00:01:56.402 --> 00:02:00.365
Trust that the bank is solid and sound

00:02:00.365 --> 00:02:02.617
is what it needs to attain every day

00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:04.077
to keep ticking along.

00:02:04.077 --> 00:02:06.246
So showing that it's able to do a deal

00:02:06.246 --> 00:02:08.373
like the one it was
trying to do with Paschi

00:02:08.373 --> 00:02:10.333
is absolutely critical.

00:02:14.587 --> 00:02:15.755
And you have Monte dei Paschi

00:02:15.755 --> 00:02:18.466
which just recently bought a big rival

00:02:18.466 --> 00:02:21.010
at the peak of the market in cash,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.179
already stretching its finances.

00:02:23.179 --> 00:02:27.934
Suddenly, it was facing another
450 million dollars potentially

00:02:27.934 --> 00:02:30.228
in a single loss, in a single investment,

00:02:30.228 --> 00:02:32.021
that it would have to book at 2008.

00:02:34.941 --> 00:02:36.609
They couldn't admit the loss

00:02:36.609 --> 00:02:38.653
that they actually had faced

00:02:38.653 --> 00:02:42.073
and so this deal was to cover that loss.

00:02:42.073 --> 00:02:45.243
The risk they were facing
was nationalization

00:02:45.243 --> 00:02:46.619
by the Italian government

00:02:46.619 --> 00:02:49.664
or control by the bank of Italy, direct.

00:02:49.664 --> 00:02:50.707
It was a prospect

00:02:50.707 --> 00:02:54.085
that management was trying
everything it possibly could

00:02:54.085 --> 00:02:55.753
to avert.

00:02:55.753 --> 00:02:58.715
They'd come up with this complex,

00:02:58.715 --> 00:03:02.802
multi-layered transaction
which, like magic,

00:03:02.802 --> 00:03:05.138
takes the loss that they
would have to crystalize

00:03:05.138 --> 00:03:06.806
at the end of 2008

00:03:06.806 --> 00:03:08.808
and spreads it out over many years

00:03:08.808 --> 00:03:10.310
in a fashion that they decide

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.104
doesn't have to be
disclosed to investors.

00:03:13.104 --> 00:03:17.609
And of course it's structured in such a way

00:03:17.609 --> 00:03:19.777
that Deutsche Bank would in the long run

00:03:19.777 --> 00:03:21.863
make a great deal of money from this.

00:03:21.863 --> 00:03:24.616
So something in the
order of 60 million euros

00:03:24.616 --> 00:03:26.367
just on that trade.

00:03:26.367 --> 00:03:27.619
The whole point of it was

00:03:27.619 --> 00:03:31.998
to violate the accounting
requirements of Italy

00:03:31.998 --> 00:03:34.292
and fool the accounting structure.

00:03:34.292 --> 00:03:37.420
Stop them having, essentially,
to account for a loss.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.421
Simple as that.

00:03:41.758 --> 00:03:44.427
It wasn't long after they struck the deal

00:03:44.427 --> 00:03:48.181
that Monte Paschi started
hurting from the transaction.

00:03:48.181 --> 00:03:49.641
&lt;font color=#FFFEC5FF&gt;Monte dei Paschi's just revealed&lt;/font&gt;

00:03:49.641 --> 00:03:52.226
&lt;font color=#FFFEC5FF&gt;it could run out of cash&lt;/font&gt;
&lt;font color=#FFFEC5FF&gt;in just four months.&lt;/font&gt;

00:03:52.226 --> 00:03:54.729
&lt;font color=#FFFEC5FF&gt;It needs to offload a&lt;/font&gt;
&lt;font color=#FFFEC5FF&gt;mountain of bad loans&lt;/font&gt;

00:03:54.729 --> 00:03:57.315
&lt;font color=#FFFEC5FF&gt;or risk being wound down.&lt;/font&gt;

00:03:57.315 --> 00:03:59.025
Yet Paschi limped along,

00:03:59.025 --> 00:04:01.027
propped up by government bailout

00:04:01.027 --> 00:04:04.447
after losses on bad loans also piled up.

00:04:04.447 --> 00:04:07.075
Astonishingly, it took
more than four years

00:04:07.075 --> 00:04:08.952
for the chicanery to come to light.

00:04:08.952 --> 00:04:11.704
Well after, regulators
from the U.S. to Italy

00:04:11.704 --> 00:04:12.872
had been alerted to it.

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:18.795
A tipster reached out and said,

00:04:18.795 --> 00:04:20.129
"I have something for you

00:04:20.129 --> 00:04:22.298
that's going to be of great interest."

00:04:22.298 --> 00:04:26.302
And there started my
reporting on Monte Paschi.

00:04:27.387 --> 00:04:30.556
Little by little the
source got comfortable

00:04:30.556 --> 00:04:33.351
in sharing more and more details

00:04:33.351 --> 00:04:34.560
about this transaction

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.354
that to be honest with you,

00:04:36.354 --> 00:04:38.398
looked really too good to be true.

00:04:39.565 --> 00:04:43.611
The material, when looked
at it in its completeness,

00:04:43.611 --> 00:04:45.905
spoke quite clearly of what was going on.

00:04:45.905 --> 00:04:50.910
This transaction, that had
allowed the world's oldest bank

00:04:51.035 --> 00:04:53.663
to basically cook its books.

00:04:53.663 --> 00:04:54.831
So what I endeavoured to do

00:04:54.831 --> 00:04:57.417
was to find experts in the field

00:04:57.417 --> 00:05:00.878
who would help stand up

00:05:00.878 --> 00:05:03.881
what these transactions were trying to do,

00:05:03.881 --> 00:05:06.759
help substantiate what
the source was telling me

00:05:06.759 --> 00:05:09.804
and one of these was
Professor Michael Dempster

00:05:09.804 --> 00:05:10.888
from Cambridge.

00:05:10.888 --> 00:05:14.267
I had seen in past complex deals,

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:16.227
but I'd never seen anything like this

00:05:16.227 --> 00:05:21.232
where basically it was shifting
cash back and forth in time.

00:05:23.401 --> 00:05:26.070
So, a simple version of how the deal worked

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.447
goes something like this.

00:05:27.447 --> 00:05:28.906
You had two parts,

00:05:28.906 --> 00:05:31.451
one was sure to win for Monte dei Paschi

00:05:31.451 --> 00:05:33.828
and one was sure to lose
for Monte dei Paschi.

00:05:34.829 --> 00:05:36.914
The part that was sure to win

00:05:36.914 --> 00:05:39.000
would generate a gain for Monte dei Paschi

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.129
which it would use to
cover up the existing loss.

00:05:43.129 --> 00:05:45.798
The part that was sure to
lose for Monte dei Paschi,

00:05:45.798 --> 00:05:47.467
the Italian bank would keep,

00:05:47.467 --> 00:05:49.761
but it wouldn't crystallize that loss

00:05:49.761 --> 00:05:50.928
right at 2008,

00:05:50.928 --> 00:05:53.806
it would spread out the
loss over many years.

00:05:53.806 --> 00:05:55.266
What was striking was the fact

00:05:55.266 --> 00:05:59.103
that the client was being
given a huge amount of money.

00:05:59.103 --> 00:06:01.606
Half a billion euros roughly speaking.

00:06:01.606 --> 00:06:03.232
We'd never seen a deal like this

00:06:03.232 --> 00:06:07.153
in which the client received
a large amount of cash

00:06:07.153 --> 00:06:09.238
at the beginning and paid it back

00:06:09.238 --> 00:06:10.948
and then some afterward.

00:06:10.948 --> 00:06:12.450
When I saw it

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:16.621
and when Elisa Martinuzzi saw it,

00:06:16.621 --> 00:06:18.664
I mean it's obvious.

00:06:19.791 --> 00:06:21.959
It's an illegal accounting device.

00:06:31.677 --> 00:06:33.262
So initially it was the Siena prosecutors

00:06:33.262 --> 00:06:35.431
that started looking
at these transactions

00:06:35.431 --> 00:06:39.185
and it went to trial
about three years ago

00:06:39.185 --> 00:06:41.270
and the verdict came in November.

00:06:47.235 --> 00:06:49.529
A Milan court convicted 13 individuals

00:06:49.529 --> 00:06:52.198
for helping Monte Paschi hide losses

00:06:52.198 --> 00:06:53.908
from its accounts.

00:06:53.908 --> 00:06:56.536
Individuals included Monte
Paschi's former chairman,

00:06:56.536 --> 00:06:57.912
Giuseppe Mussari,

00:06:57.912 --> 00:07:00.289
and Deutsche Bank's former
head of global rates,

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:01.415
Michele Faissola.

00:07:05.378 --> 00:07:07.547
The judges also ordered the banks

00:07:07.547 --> 00:07:11.217
to pay fines and set aside amounts

00:07:11.217 --> 00:07:12.635
that totalled 160 million.

00:07:13.845 --> 00:07:15.263
And it ordered further inquiries

00:07:15.263 --> 00:07:17.640
into some of the individuals
who had testified.

00:07:18.808 --> 00:07:20.810
These are by a number of measures

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:22.770
the most significant convictions

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.730
relating to the financial crisis.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:28.609
You haven't had chairmen of institutions,

00:07:28.609 --> 00:07:31.821
let alone 13 senior managers

00:07:31.821 --> 00:07:36.325
across three institutions
face jail sentences

00:07:36.325 --> 00:07:38.077
for their wrongdoing.

00:07:38.077 --> 00:07:40.788
But of course this is
convicted in Italian court.

00:07:40.788 --> 00:07:42.748
They don't have to go to jail

00:07:42.748 --> 00:07:44.542
unless they have a second trial

00:07:44.542 --> 00:07:45.668
in which they essentially

00:07:45.668 --> 00:07:47.211
can put them in jail.

00:07:47.211 --> 00:07:52.216
So whilst they've been
besmirched, so to speak,

00:07:52.341 --> 00:07:53.634
their reputations,

00:07:53.634 --> 00:07:57.555
it's not terribly huge punishment.

00:07:57.555 --> 00:07:59.390
My initial response to the verdicts

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:02.059
was that they were not stiff enough

00:08:02.059 --> 00:08:04.270
and they should of gone another layer up

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:07.899
to in fact the head of
the investment bank.

00:08:12.737 --> 00:08:13.571
I'm not so sure

00:08:13.571 --> 00:08:15.364
that the public can really be confident

00:08:15.364 --> 00:08:17.825
that we're not gonna see
another Monte Paschi.

00:08:17.825 --> 00:08:19.869
The situation is
essentially the same today

00:08:19.869 --> 00:08:21.037
as it was before.

00:08:21.037 --> 00:08:22.538
A lot more complicated

00:08:22.538 --> 00:08:25.625
because the regulators have
tried to make it better,

00:08:25.625 --> 00:08:27.126
but they've failed basically.

00:08:28.711 --> 00:08:30.087
Having spoken to regulators

00:08:30.087 --> 00:08:32.548
about the lessons from
the financial crisis,

00:08:32.548 --> 00:08:35.384
it's pretty clear where
the weaknesses still lie.

00:08:36.594 --> 00:08:38.888
Generally, on all the
reforms that have been done

00:08:38.888 --> 00:08:41.182
since the financial crisis,

00:08:41.182 --> 00:08:42.225
we've made them better,

00:08:42.225 --> 00:08:44.101
but we probably haven't
made them good enough.

00:08:44.101 --> 00:08:47.146
So there's really not
been fundamental changes.

00:08:47.146 --> 00:08:48.606
We've kind of made these improvements

00:08:48.606 --> 00:08:50.274
around the edges.

00:08:50.274 --> 00:08:52.693
I think people should still be concerned.

00:08:52.693 --> 00:08:54.320
I think there's this kind of assumption

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:56.572
that it's yesterday's news

00:08:56.572 --> 00:08:59.617
and I think that's probably ill-advised

00:08:59.617 --> 00:09:01.327
because I think there's
still some real fragility

00:09:01.327 --> 00:09:02.161
in the system.

00:09:03.037 --> 00:09:05.081
As a senior investment banking executive

00:09:05.081 --> 00:09:06.374
recently told me,

00:09:06.374 --> 00:09:08.042
his chief financial officer

00:09:08.042 --> 00:09:10.169
would not be able to challenge him

00:09:10.169 --> 00:09:12.713
on what's in his books.

00:09:12.713 --> 00:09:15.758
You'd need a PhD to understand
a lot of these transactions

00:09:15.758 --> 00:09:17.718
and the people in charge

00:09:17.718 --> 00:09:20.680
are often - don't have those PhDs.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:23.057
It is not an awful lot
to be confident about

00:09:23.057 --> 00:09:24.433
what's really lurking

00:09:24.433 --> 00:09:27.895
inside financial
institutions' balance sheets.

00:09:27.895 --> 00:09:30.856
And it remains just as difficult

00:09:30.856 --> 00:09:33.359
for the outside world to get into them.

00:09:33.359 --> 00:09:34.360
Yes, it's true,

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:35.820
the higher management often

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:39.115
doesn't understand much at all actually.

00:09:39.115 --> 00:09:41.200
Which of course is a serious problem

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:42.952
when there is a crisis.

00:09:42.952 --> 00:09:45.037
Not unlike what we saw in the lead up

00:09:45.037 --> 00:09:46.664
to the global financial crisis

00:09:46.664 --> 00:09:49.458
where it was mortgage
debt that had ballooned,

00:09:49.458 --> 00:09:51.585
this time it's corporate debt.

00:09:51.585 --> 00:09:53.754
The market has grown enormously

00:09:53.754 --> 00:09:58.134
and I believe that
there's a recession coming

00:09:58.134 --> 00:09:59.802
or a market crash coming

00:09:59.802 --> 00:10:01.971
and it's a government matter,

00:10:01.971 --> 00:10:03.389
it's a political matter,

00:10:04.265 --> 00:10:07.184
and whether or not the politicians

00:10:07.184 --> 00:10:09.145
will be able to sort something out,

00:10:09.145 --> 00:10:10.855
I'm very pessimistic about that.

